From 23101a332cf7b85e943ddc6aa7fd4d21a87315fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 21:41:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Carry device keys and to-device messages across
 federation

The client-server key endpoints only help when both devices are on the
same homeserver. On a phone mesh they never are: every phone runs its own
Neutrino, so every peer you want to encrypt to is a remote user, and a
purely local key directory answers every query with an empty object.

Adds the federation half:

- POST /_matrix/federation/v1/user/keys/query
- POST /_matrix/federation/v1/user/keys/claim
- GET  /_matrix/federation/v1/user/devices/{userId}
- m.direct_to_device EDUs, both directions

The client-server /keys/query, /keys/claim and /sendToDevice handlers now
split their request by the server that owns each user, answer for our own
and ask (or tell) the owning server for the rest. A peer we cannot reach
lands in `failures` rather than failing the whole call, so a client still
encrypts to everyone it did get keys for.

Inbound EDUs are handled past the whole-transaction dedup, so a peer
retrying a transaction does not re-deliver a room key. Messages addressed
to users we do not own are dropped, never relayed: forwarding would let
any peer inject to-device traffic under our origin.

Known limitation, called out in the code: outbound EDUs bypass the durable
outbox (which stores PDUs), so delivery is best-effort. A peer out of range
when a key is shared does not get it later; the client's re-share on the
next send to an unknown device is the recovery path. Making this durable is
the obvious next step.

Verified against two servers on loopback: a remote device queried, its
one-time keys claimed exactly once each, and a room key delivered to the
other server's /sync. Twelve tests cover the routes, the ownership scoping,
claim exhaustion, EDU dedup and the auth gate.
